 ('table',0),
 ('h2','How a fixed foil gets thrust from waves'),
 ('p','Why: A wave turns the incoming flow upwards and downwards. Lift acts at right angles to that flow. During part of the cycle, lift therefore has a forward component. The cycle can leave a small average thrust, even though the foil stays fixed. In short waves, the flow response falls behind and this forward effect becomes weaker.'),
 ('sketch',None),
 ('small','How sure we are: The 95% Student-t ranges show cycle scatter, but exclude numerical error and dependence between cycles.'),
 ('small','What to do next: Check W05 with smaller time steps.'),
 ('page','How the models explain the forces'),
 ('p','What we found: The Sears model has the lowest RMSE, 0.818 N. RMSE measures the typical size of a model’s force error. The quasi-steady and travelling-wave errors are 6.30 N and 0.935 N. R² measures how much of the force variation the model explains.'),
 ('p','Why: Quasi-steady means that the flow responds immediately to each change. The Sears function describes weaker and later lift when a flow disturbance passes the foil. Reduced frequency compares flow travel time along the foil with the wave period. The travelling-wave model also includes the wave’s own motion. [1–4]'),
 ('image',12),
 ('caption','Figure 1. Left: measured force reduction compared with the quasi-steady reference. Right: all 14 cases and an origin fit with R² of 0.850. An origin fit passes through zero. A frozen gust stays unchanged while it passes the foil. Nominal means using target wave height. Angle amplitude is the predicted size of the wave-driven change in flow direction. Bars show the cycle scatter from Table 1. The physical models use no fitted force parameters.'),
 ('small','How sure we are: Wavelength changes both steepness and wave travel, so the trend does not separate their effects. Steepness compares wave height with wavelength.'),
 ('small','What to do next: Compare predictions using the local measured wave height.'),
 ('h2','Local flow before the foil'),
 ('p','What we found: A 0.0500 s delay raises R² from 0.677 to 0.967. A wave-travel delay of 0.0390 s gives 0.917.'),
 ('small','Why: We use flow direction and speed before the water reaches the foil. The attack angle lies between the incoming flow and the chord. The chord joins the front and rear edges of the foil. Advection means transport by the mean flow. The 0.0500 s delay allows for that travel time.'),
 ('image',15),
 ('caption','Figure 2. We fit three cycles and predict the remaining cycle. Wave phase identifies the position within a cycle. We align the cycles at the foil probe, x = 0. The curves show changes in horizontal force.'),
 ('small','How sure we are: We cannot separate travel effects from flow memory because none of 120 points matches both angle and speed. Flow memory means that the present force depends on past motion.'),
 ('small','What to do next: Compare pressure and friction forces at the same wave phase. Pressure pushes on the surface. Friction acts along it.'),
 ('page','Practical meaning and the next checks'),
 ('h2','Comparing the same load'),
 ('p','What we found: Waves increase mean Fz by 9.88–19.2 N. Equal-load means comparing cases at the same mean vertical force. The W04e wave fit gives a force sensitivity of 0.0511 N/N.'),
 ('small','Why: A change in vertical force can also change horizontal force. The correction reaches at least 30% of the signal for W01–W03 and W04j. It reaches 70% for W02 and 35% for W04h. A polar shows how lift and drag change with attack angle. Laminar flow has little turbulent mixing. A NACA 66 foil can have an almost flat drag slope in its laminar low-drag range. SST is the turbulence model used in our simulations. [14]'),
 ('small','How sure we are: Our sensitivity comes from a wave fit, rather than a measured steady polar.'),
 ('small','What to do next: Obtain that steady polar at the same mean vertical load.'),
 ('h2','Testing a flexible trailing edge'),
 ('p','What we found: Unloading removes 11.1, 23.9 and 41.0% of the W04e saving at κ values of 0.3, 0.5 and 0.7. Unloading reduces the change in attack angle. Stiffening increases that change. The largest stiffening gain is 0.0981 N near κ of 0.190. The Fz amplitude grows by 17.8%. Amplitude measures the size of a repeated rise and fall.'),
 ('small','Why: The trailing edge is the rear edge of the foil. Compliance means bending under load. We represented bending by changing the angle used in the model. κ sets the strength of this imposed change. Stronger lift increases the forward pull, but the force along the flow grows faster. [9–13]'),
 ('small','How sure we are: The gain is smaller than the model error and uses angles outside the calibration range. Calibration means using data to set the model coefficients.'),
 ('small','What to do next: Test flap timing in the Mini-VOF phase scan.'),
