EXTRA_A=[
 'C01 has horizontal force of 27.0 N and buoyancy-corrected vertical force of 1.22 × 10³ N. Its fixed-axis Fz/Fx ratio is 45.1. The domain axes stay fixed when the incoming flow turns.',
 'The largest horizontal-force change, W04, is −0.827% of calm Fz. The calm M12–M13 force difference is 1.14 N. Those cases also used different time steps and settling states. W02’s saving is smaller than that difference. W01 and W03 have savings of similar size.',
 'W04i and W04j passed five-cycle checks on 9 November 2026. Both passed the same numerical-health and cycles 2–5 repeatability rules as the other cases. We also checked native SIM/CSV endpoints, C01/PRE history and clean saved exits.',
 'We measure Hloc at x of −0.5 m, except W01 at −6 m. The equal-cycle and duration-weighted averages are both retained in the data files.',
]

# Existing appendix paragraph IDs, from the immutable v4 DOCX.
APP={
31:'Table 3. Setup, mesh and force definitions from the archived files.',
33:'HRIC upper Courant 1.00 lies below every recorded interface maximum in Table 4. Affected interface cells therefore use an upwind contribution. This is a more diffusive numerical method that can weaken waves. The attenuated cases have local-to-target height ratios near 0.96–0.99. The height-squared force scaling predicts up to 8.14% less force reduction. The maxima do not show how much of the interface uses upwind. The foil also changes the local wave.',
34:'The saved area means of y+ are 0.937 and 0.950. These agree within 7% with the ITTC estimate of 1.00 using the full 1.53 μm layer. The cell-centre estimate is 0.500. Local extremes differ by more than 50%. ITTC assumes flat-plate mean shear and uniform spacing. Foil pressure gradients and nonuniform wall-cell spacing change the local result. Only 0.117% of foil area exceeds y+ of 1. The maximum 255 occurs on HF_012_wall. The audit did not save its position, so we cannot assign x/c, surface side or edge location.',
35:'Cycles 2–5 agree within each run, including retained continuation segments. Every accepted regular case has five complete measured cycles. We exclude the first cycle from all means and repeatability checks. Surface crossings define the cycles. Published Qx uses only the original C01 reference.',
36:'We subtract the 22.7 N buoyancy correction from the stored vertical force to obtain Fz. Fx is positive along +x. The force-difference and Qx definitions are in Appendix B. Qx describes horizontal resistance along fixed domain axes.',
38:'Table 4. CFLi and CFLmax are the largest recorded values of spatial maxima, rather than averages. CFLi covers cells with water fraction between 0.01 and 0.99. CFLmax covers the whole fluid region. We report saved-step positions separately.',
40:'The W04e saved-step global Courant maximum is 17.4. It occurs at coordinates (0.353, −0.0676) m in x and z. This point lies 0.0273 m from a wall, near the foil. It is not necessarily in the first prism layer. The saved-step interface maximum is 1.89 at (−11.9, 1.91) m. It lies in the incoming free-surface wave. The history does not retain positions of earlier maxima.',
41:'The orbital-angle calculation uses 2 m submergence and the equations in Appendix B. Orbital-angle amplitude describes the predicted wave-driven change in flow direction. For target wave height of 1.5 m, it spans 2.09–2.27°. The encounter frequency changes substantially across these cases. Shorter waves give less force reduction relative to the quasi-steady prediction. Wavelength also changes steepness and wave propagation. Local-height scaling uses the measured height, which includes foil-induced flow.',
42:'Table 5. Results for all 14 cases using target height and local measured height. “Nominal” columns mean target height. Local-height predictions use the squared height ratio. We refit the origin-fit slope for the changed input.',
44:'Using local height lowers the Sears RMSE from 0.818 to 0.701 N. The main conclusion stays the same. Classical unsteady models explain much of the trend. Height calibration still mixes numerical diffusion with the foil’s effect on the wave.',
45:'The origin fit for 14 cases has a slope of −5.66 × 10³ N/rad². This fit only describes the data. The earlier 12-case fit had R² of 0.839, before we added W04i and W04j.',
47:'H⁽²⁾ denotes a Hankel function, while J denotes a Bessel function. The frozen-gust response multiplies the quasi-steady force by the squared magnitude of the Sears function. [1–2]',
49:'The travelling-wave model separates encounter frequency from spatial wavelength. The following expressions use the zero-motion limit from [3], Eq. 2.16 and Appendix A16, A25–A26.',
52:'Matching the spatial and encounter scales recovers the frozen-gust Sears result. These inviscid thin-foil models omit camber, viscosity and free-surface feedback. Free-surface theory allows both wave resistance and thrust. [5]',
54:'Table 6. The bracket gives [observed force change, wave-fit equal-load scenario], in that order. It is not a confidence interval. AR columns keep the observed 2D force change fixed. They add calm induced drag using span efficiency of 0.9.',
56:'The calm induced-drag additions at aspect ratios 4, 6 and 8 are 34.0, 22.6 and 17.0 N. A finite-span foil loses energy through tip vortices. This adds induced drag. Aspect ratio describes how long and narrow the foil is. Span efficiency describes how well the lift distribution limits tip-vortex losses. For W04, the same 2D force reduction becomes 16.5, 20.3 and 22.9% of these larger calm drag baselines. We assume span efficiency of 0.9. The CSV also estimates how the measured mean vertical force changes mean induced drag. It excludes the extra mean-square contribution from force oscillations and vessel motions.',
57:'The projected force sensitivity is 0.0511 N/N. We divide the mean horizontal-force derivative by the mean vertical-force derivative at fixed recorded inflow. The parallel-only sensitivity is 0.0758. The bracket runs from no trim correction to the correction transferred from this wave fit. Trim means changing the mean attack angle to restore the mean vertical load. This is the wave-fit sensitivity described in Table 2.',
58:'Table 7. Angle-change scenarios at the same modelled mean vertical load. A positive force change reduces the rigid-model saving. κ controls the imposed angle response, rather than material stiffness.',
60:'We define force amplitude as half the difference between the 99th and 1st percentiles. The angle-law equations are in Appendix B. Linear unloading acts throughout the cycle. The high-angle law acts only above the mean angle. Stiffening increases the angle variation. We adjust trim until mean Fz matches the rigid model within the numerical root tolerance. Recorded speed and flow direction stay fixed.',
62:'Table 8. Horizontal-force harmonics at the foil and the difference between averaging windows. A1–A3 are Fourier amplitudes in N. δwindow compares the foil-phase mean with the published Q-window mean.',
64:'The second harmonic measures departures from a simple sine wave. A2/A1 measures its size relative to the first harmonic. Phase and sign also affect where the waveform flattens. We use a common phase grid at the foil. Older records use upstream gauges and wave-travel estimates to rebuild that phase. The CSV files include vertical-force harmonics and both exact averaging-window endpoints.',
65:'The largest difference between Q-window and foil-phase means across 14 cases is 0.00961 N. Their mean forces therefore agree closely. We keep both window definitions and their different endpoints.',
67:'W08bi combines two first-order components. Their heights and wavelengths are 0.500 m with 20.0 m, and 1.00 m with 30.0 m. Both have phase 0°. The predicted and archived beat periods are both 2.85 s. A beat is the slow rise and fall caused by combining different wave frequencies. The saved diagnostic ends at 25.7 s. We average three beat envelopes over 17.1–25.7 s. We exclude this diagnostic from the 14 regular-wave fits.',
68:'The diagnostic window gives horizontal-force change of −5.67 N and vertical-force change of 15.2 N. The fitted local component heights are 0.475 and 0.986 m. The independent polar model and fitted W04e model use different inputs. We therefore keep their results separate.',
70:'We sample W04e inflow at x of −0.9 m before the foil. We shift this record by the fixed travel delay. The velocity components give flow direction and attack angle. We rotate the forces into directions along and across that flow. We fit a quadratic parallel-force coefficient and a linear normal-force coefficient. We then rotate the predicted forces back to the fixed domain axes. Appendix B gives the equations.',
71:'Table 9. We fit three cycles and predict the remaining cycle, repeating this for each cycle. We set the delays from travel times, without selecting them to improve force agreement.',
73:'The 0.0500 s delay uses probe-to-leading-edge distance and mean flow speed. The wave-phase delay uses that distance, the measured encounter period and wavelength. We interpolate the recorded velocities in physical time. This retains timing differences between cycles. We exclude the repeated 360° endpoint from each fit.',
74:'The compliance model uses cycles 2–5. Its parallel-force coefficient is quadratic in attack angle. Its normal-force coefficient is linear. Appendix B gives the equations, with attack angle in degrees. The coefficients are stored at full precision in results.json. Leaving out a cycle tests consistency within W04e.',
75:'None of the 120 opposite-branch phase points matches both angle and speed. The matching limits are ±0.0500° in angle and ±0.0500 m/s in speed. The branches must differ by at least 30.0° in phase. Travel delay and speed mismatch can create a loop in an attack-angle versus force plot.',
77:'Figure 3. Mean horizontal-force change from the rigid W04e model after restoring mean vertical load. The curves describe imposed angle changes.',
78:'Pressure and viscous forces over cycles 2–5 are needed to explain what remains. Native field histories contain only their saved segments and sampling times. A force maximum or minimum may fall between field snapshots. We did not run a new field-reconstruction calculation.',
80:'We test 399 scenarios. Gain g ranges from 0 to 2 in steps of 0.1. Phase ranges from −90° to 90° in steps of 10°. Appendix B gives the angle law. Trim restores mean Fz to the rigid value. Speed and force-projection angle remain fixed against changes in the imposed phase.',
82:'Figure 4. Change in mean horizontal force from the rigid model. Zero lies at the centre of the colour scale. The negative range, −0.1 to 0 N, has its own linear scale. The positive range runs from 0 to 15 N. This expands the small negative region. Hatching marks effective angles above 3.41° or below −0.38°.',
84:'Figure 5. Vertical-force amplitude from the same scenarios. We use half the difference between the 99th and 1st percentiles. Hatching has the same meaning as in Figure 4. Each scenario keeps mean Fz unchanged.',
85:'The rebuilt rigid-angle range is −0.380° to 3.45°. The requested limits are −0.38° to 3.41°. The rigid reference therefore also touches the hatched region. We apply these limits directly to all 209 flagged grid points.',
86:'Within those angle limits, the best nonzero-phase point gives a 0.219 N penalty. Its gain is 0.900 and phase is 10.0°. Including angles outside those limits gives a best penalty of 0.0201 N. No phase-shifted point gives a saving. The held-out RMSE is 2.68 N. It exceeds the in-sample RMSE by 0.000263 N. These error measures do not give confidence limits for a mean design saving.',
88:'The grid minimum at zero phase is partly built into the model. The angle law acts immediately while recorded speed and flow direction stay fixed against phase changes. For a simple harmonic input, forward projection gives an even phase response. The largest difference between positive and negative phases is 0.0440 N. Measured harmonics, finite windows and speed–angle correlations make the map only approximately symmetric. This model result does not settle the best physical flap phase.',
89:'Across reduced frequencies 0.137–0.316, the Theodorsen phase ranges from −15.1° to −13.2°. The Sears phase ranges from −12.8° to −11.0°. Appendix B states the harmonic sign convention. These phases describe the circulation and gust responses. They do not give the phase of the total nonlinear CFD force. A flap that moves ahead of the wave could help offset those delays. [1–2]',
90:'The next test is Mini-VOF with a prescribed flap motion. Place the hinge at chord position x/c of 0.80. Link the flap motion to the incoming wave and scan its phase. Compare cases at the same mean Fz.',
91:'A gain above 1 needs a response that increases the load. A hinge behind the centre of pressure could allow this. The centre of pressure is the effective point where the pressure force acts. Fluid torque could then overcome structural stiffness and cause divergence. Hinge position, inertia and damping set the response timing. At reduced frequency near 0.32, this quasi-steady model omits curvature change, elastic work and damping.',
101:'Sources [1–5] describe classical unsteady forces and wave thrust. Sources [6–8] connect these ideas to hydrofoils and vessel control. The bibliography states where full text was available. The calculation scripts reproduce the equations and numerical predictions. We transfer no numerical gain from another experiment to this campaign.',
110:'Saved-field audits of C01 and W04e used 0 new time steps. They provide phase properties, HRIC limits, turbulence-model names and foil y+. The present extract does not include vertical domain bounds.',
111:'Acceptance of cycles 2–5 leaves several missing checks listed in Table 2. We still need incident-only inflow and a steady equal-load polar for this geometry. We also need controlled mesh/time-step refinement and a quantified reflection coefficient. The calm mesh difference mixes several changes. Existing active-flap XFOIL data use a different calibration.',
112:'The 14 accepted cases leave some effects unmeasured. These include pressure and viscous contributions by phase, independent-run uncertainty, finite-span NWAR, structural work and damping. CPU-only verification costs are also unknown. Imported W04i and W04j exports have no native field histories. Their force records, probes and final SIMs remain available.',
114:'The data folder retains all 14 accepted cases and the original C01 reference. README.txt explains the data history and reproduction steps. Source paths and hashes are in data/lineage.json. Numerical checks are in checks.json.',
}

# These are relocated expressions already present in v4, not new model equations.
FORMULAE=[
 'Orbital angle: θa = (H/2)ω₀ exp(−kwh)/U; kw = 2π/λ. The submergence h is 2 m.',
 'Local-height prediction = target-height prediction × (Hloc/H)². The origin-fit slope is refitted for this changed input.',
 'Force differences: ΔFx = Fxwave − FxC01; ΔFz = Fzwave − FzC01; Qx = ΔFx/qS.',
 'Equal-load sensitivity: ΔFx − (dFx/dFz)ΔFz; dFx/dFz = 0.0511 N/N.',
 'W04e stiffening fit: ΔFx(κ) = −1.03κ + 2.72κ² N. The forward term is −Fn sin β; the parallel term includes b2α².',
 'Induced drag: Di = qS Cz₀²/(πeAR), with e = 0.9.',
 'Local inflow: β = atan2(Uz, Ux); α = β − chord angle; local force scale is qlocalS.',
 'Force coefficients: Cparallel = b0 + b1α + b2α²; Cnormal = a0 + a1α. Here α is in degrees.',
 'Linear unloading: αeff = ᾱ + (1 − κ)(α − ᾱ) + trim. Stiffening replaces (1 − κ) with (1 + κ).',
 'Phase scan: αeff = ᾱ + g[α(t − φ/ωe) − ᾱ] + trim; ωe = 2π/Te. Here φ is in radians.',
 'Harmonic sign convention: exp(+iωt). The Theodorsen and Sears phases therefore describe lags when negative.',
 'Origin fit: ΔFx = sθa². The model fit only describes the present data.',
 'Travelling-wave scales: κw = kwc/2; F = Re C; G = Im C. Setting κw = σ recovers |S|².',
]

